          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            has the property that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            maps nearby points to nearby points, which may be formalised by first picking one point, then considering how nearby you want the points to be, then picking another point sufficiently nearby.
          %p
            The concept of
            %em uniformly continuous map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is based on the same intuition but a different formalisation: first you pick how nearby you want the points to be, then you pick two points sufficiently nearby. This results in a stronger criterion, definable in a less general context.
          %p Traditionally this is formalized
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+fields"} Archimedean fields
                (def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BetweenArchimedeanFields"}
                below)
            %li
              %p
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
                (def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BetweenMetricSpaces"}
                below)
          %p but the definition makes sense more generally
          %ul
            %li
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+spaces"} uniform spaces
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BetweenUniformSpaces"}
              below)
          %p and in fact
          %ul
